{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788348717406,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"id":"prt_061e3d95c001fqnlelU6o0WXDK","messageID":"msg_061e3cf9b001kHp8tjYdrL7JaT","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348719312,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_a66e0249630b4bb4a4970d16","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-cannbench-kernel\">\n# Skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel\n\n# pyasc v2 CANNBench kernel workflow\n\nUse this skill only for the vendored benchmark under\n`integrations/cannbench/`. Its compact module contract overrides the generic\nstandalone-kernel workflow.\n\n## Non-negotiable contract\n\n- Treat `tasks/<op>/proto.yaml`, `golden.py`, and all 20 entries in\n  `cases.yaml` as one indivisible specification.\n- Produce one `candidate.py` with the exact top-level callable and defaults.\n- Use `torch` only for allocation, metadata views, and launches. Numerical work\n  belongs in one or more `@asc2.jit` kernels.\n- Import and call `ensure_npu_platform` from `._pyasc_runtime`.\n- Target `Ascend950PR_9599`, at most 72 vector cores, and UB capacity 253952 B.\n- For the pinned runtime use `asc2.global_tensor`, `asc2.copy_in`, and\n  `asc2.copy_out`; use `real_shape` for tails. Do not substitute the older\n  `tensor/load/store` spelling.\n- A production candidate normally uses bare `@asc2.jit` or an evidenced\n  allocation option. `always_compile=True` is a development aid, not a\n  submission requirement.\n\n## Required workflow\n\n1. Read the three task files and summarize every dtype, rank, attribute, list\n   arity, special value, and boundary shape. Do not optimize for one example.\n2. Read [operator patterns](references/operator-patterns.md), selecting only\n   the target operator section.\n3. Design host dispatch, kernel specializations, tails, dtype conversions, UB\n   budget, and numerical behavior before writing code.\n4. Implement the exact callable. Run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` and\n   the worker static contract check.\n5. Run the exact-v2 local compile gate for that operator as described in\n   [local validation](references/local-validation.md). A compile failure is\n   measured feedback; repair it and re-run all 20 cases.\n6. Have a different available model review the task, design, candidate, and\n   local report. Record both model IDs, OpenCode session IDs, loaded skill\n   source hashes, prompt/design/candidate hashes, and gate results.\n\nNever claim numerical correctness or performance from the local compile gate.\nOnly camodel execution can provide local numerical evidence; only CANNBench on\nthe real NPU is the acceptance/performance oracle.\n\n## Evidence labels\n\nUse exactly one label for every conclusion:\n\n- `verified-local-compile`: all task routes lower through pinned pyasc v2.\n- `verified-camodel`: outputs were executed and compared to the task golden.\n- `verified-cannbench`: official hardware result and case count are recorded.\n- `suspected`: evidence is incomplete or the root cause is inferred.\n\nDo not promote a locally qualified candidate into the canonical submission\npackage without numerical evidence. Do not spend a submission credit unless\nthe user explicitly enables remote evaluation.\n\n## Known limitations\n\nBefore using a workaround, consult\n[pyasc v2 blockers](references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md). Preserve full case\ncoverage when a case exposes a compiler limit; never remove or shrink the case\nto make a gate pass.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/local-validation.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel","dir":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel","time":{"start":1788348719304,"end":1788348719311}},"id":"prt_061e3e01e0011BHzC4vr13Wuw6","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","messageID":"msg_061e3cf9b001kHp8tjYdrL7JaT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348719652,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_ab4bb79d56404d538d779956","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-syntax-constraints\">\n# Skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints\n\n# pyasc Syntax Constraints\n\nSyntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\nsource of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n`SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\nagainst that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n`asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n\n## Overview\n\npyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n\n## Supported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Example | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| Attribute access | `nums.__len__()` | |\n| `while` loop | `while i < n: ...` | |\n| `for` loop | `for i in asc2.range(n): ...` | Use `asc2.range()` inside kernel, not `range()` |\n| Binary operators | `a + b * c` | `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `//`, `**`, `&`, `\\|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` |\n| Unary operators | `~a`, `-a`, `not a` | |\n| `tuple` | `return x, y, z` | |\n| `list` | `nums = [a, b, c]` | |\n| `pass` | `pass` | |\n| Ternary `if` | `ans = x if x > 0 else 0` | |\n| `if`/`elif`/`else` | Full conditional blocks | |\n| Constants | `a = 1; b = True` | |\n| Comparisons | `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=` | |\n| Function calls | `func()` | Must be `@asc2.jit`-decorated or asc2 built-in |\n| Boolean logic | `and`, `or` | |\n| Augmented assign | `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=` | |\n| Assignment | `x = expr` | |\n| Annotated assign | `count: int = 3` | |\n| f-strings + assert | `assert cond, f\"msg {val}\"` | |\n| Subscript | `nums[0]` | |\n| Slice | `tensor[offset:]` | |\n\n## Unsupported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Status | Workaround |\n|--------|--------|------------|\n| Nested functions | Not supported | Define separate `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `global` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| `return` from kernel | Not supported | Kernels write to output tensors; device functions may return |\n| `return` inside `if`/`for` | Not supported | Use top-level return only in device functions |\n| `continue` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `print()` | Not supported | Use `assert` with f-strings |\n| `with open(...)` | Not supported | Do file I/O outside JIT |\n| `raise` | Not supported | Use `assert` instead |\n| `try`/`except` | Not supported | Handle errors outside JIT |\n| `yield` / `yield from` | Not supported | No generators |\n| `lambda` | Not supported | Define named functions |\n| `break` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `import` / `from...import` | Not supported | All imports outside JIT |\n| `async def` / `async with` | Not supported | No async |\n| `nonlocal` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| Class methods as kernels | Not supported | Use module-level functions |\n| If-scoped variables | Restricted | Initialize before `if`; use `asc.ConstExpr` for compile-time branching |\n| Unsigned int comparison | Restricted | Use `np.int64` or truthiness check instead |\n\n## Type Constraints\n\n### Kernel parameter types (runtime)\n\n| Supported | Not supported |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| `bool`, `int`, `float` | `str` |\n| `numpy` scalars and `ndarray` | `tuple`, `list`, `dict` |\n| `torch.Tensor` (NPU mode only) | Complex objects |\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` | |\n\n### Compile-time parameters\n\nUse `asc.ConstExpr[T]` for values that must be resolved at compile time:\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    # tile_size is resolved at compile time and included in the JIT cache key\n    ...\n```\n\n### Built-in whitelist inside JIT\n\nOnly these built-ins are available: `dict`, `float`, `int`, `isinstance`, `issubclass`, `len`, `list`, `range`, `repr`, `str`, `tuple`, `type`\n\nNote: For iteration inside `@asc2.jit`, use `asc2.range()` instead of `range()`.\nDo not assume optional keywords are portable between pyasc snapshots.\n\n### Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\n\n- **M/N/K tile loops are compile-time.** When the trip count is an\n  `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m/n/k tiles), a plain Python `for i in range(...)`\n  is fully unrolled at JIT time — that is the intended form for the loop that must\n  keep an operand resident in `L0A`/`L0B`. Use `asc2.range(...,\n  unroll_factor=2)` only on the *inner* tile loop you want software-pipelined\n  (double-buffered).\n- **Overlap (`gm_barrier=False`) doubles the pipelined L0 buffer.** The 2-deep\n  tile must fit half the L0 capacity (≤ 32 KiB for L0A/L0B, ≤ 64 KiB for the f32\n  L0C). A full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is 64 KiB and overflows when doubled\n  (`L0B overflow: 65536 bytes available, 131072 used`); shrink the tile (e.g.\n  `N_TILE=64`) before relying on overlap.\n- **Cube tile placement is explicit:** load/`asc2.copy` operands to\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L0A` (left) / `L0B` (right); stage from GM via\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L1` for reuse; the `@` result lands in L0C as f32 — cast with\n  `.to(asc2.float16)` before `asc2.store` if a 16-bit output is wanted.\n\n## Kernel vs Device Function Rules\n\n| Rule | Kernel function | Device function |\n|------|----------------|-----------------|\n| Launched from | Host via `kernel[core_num](...)` | Other `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `return` value | Not allowed | Allowed |\n| `@asc2.jit(...)` options | Effective (always_compile, etc.) | Ignored |\n| Nesting | Cannot be nested | Cannot be nested |\n\n## References\n\n- [Supported Syntax Details](references/supported-syntax.md)\n- [Unsupported Syntax Details](references/unsupported-syntax.md)\n- [Type Constraints](references/type-constraints.md)\n- [ConstExpr Guide](references/constexpr-guide.md)\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/supported-syntax.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/type-constraints.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/constexpr-guide.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/unsupported-syntax.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints","dir":"/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints","time":{"start":1788348719644,"end":1788348719651}},"id":"prt_061e3e1330019lIjWLIkIvwLTQ","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","messageID":"msg_061e3cf9b001kHp8tjYdrL7JaT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348720353,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_b9513fecb63d480085b9873e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **ForeachNorm**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `foreach_norm` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # ForeachNorm 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: ForeachNorm 算子对输入张量列表（TensorList）的每个张量分别进行范数运算，支持多种范数阶数。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - 梯度裁剪中的梯度范数计算\n19: - 优化器中的参数范数监控\n20: - 模型正则化中的权重范数约束\n21: \n22: **算子特征**：\n23: - 难度等级：L1（Reduction）\n24: - 输入为张量列表，对每个张量独立计算范数，输出为标量张量列表\n25: - 支持 ND 格式输入\n26: \n27: ## 2. 算子定义\n28: \n29: ### 数学公式\n30: \n31: **通用 p 范数**：\n32: \n33: $$\n34: y = \\left(\\sum_i |x_i|^p\\right)^{1/p}\n35: $$\n36: \n37: ### 常见范数\n38: \n39: | 范数阶数 (scalar) | 公式 | 含义 |\n40: |-------------------|------|------|\n41: | 0 | $\\sum_i \\mathbb{1}(x_i \\neq 0)$ | L0 范数（非零元素个数） |\n42: | 1 | $\\sum_i \\|x_i\\|$ | L1 范数（绝对值之和） |\n43: | 2 | $\\sqrt{\\sum_i x_i^2}$ | L2 范数（欧氏距离） |\n44: | inf | $\\max_i \\|x_i\\|$ | 无穷范数（最大绝对值） |\n45: \n46: ## 3. 接口规范\n47: \n48: ### 算子原型\n49: \n50: ```python\n51: cann_bench.foreach_norm(Tensor[] x, float scalar) -> Tensor[] y\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ### 输入参数说明\n55: \n56: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n57: |------|------|--------|------|\n58: | x | Tensor[] | 必选 | 输入张量列表（TensorList） |\n59: | scalar | float | 必选 | 范数阶数 |\n60: \n61: ### 输出\n62: \n63: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n64: |------|-------|-------|------|\n65: | y | 每个元素为标量张量 | 与输入 dtype 相同 | 每个输入张量的范数结果列表 |\n66: \n67: ### 数据类型\n68: \n69: | 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n70: |-----------|-----------|\n71: | float16 | float16 |\n72: | float32 | float32 |\n73: | bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n74: \n75: ### 规则与约束\n76: \n77: - 输入为 TensorList，列表中每个张量独立计算范数\n78: - 列表中各张量的 dtype 须一致\n79: - `scalar` 支持正数、负数、0、inf 等值\n80: - 负阶范数要求输入元素不为零\n81: \n82: ### 支持范围\n83: \n84: 输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n85: \n86: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n87: |---|---|---|\n88: | TensorList 长度（`L`） | 1 ~ 64 | cases.csv 实测 1 ~ 4 |\n89: | 每个张量维度数 | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1D ~ 5D |\n90: | 每个张量各维大小 | 1 ~ 1048576 | cases.csv 各维实测 2 ~ 1000003 |\n91: | 每个张量元素总数 | 1 ~ 48M | cases.csv 每个张量实测 ~1M ~ ~47M |\n92: | `scalar`（范数阶数） | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 -1.0 ~ 5.0（含 inf）；负阶或 0 阶时输入元素须非零 |\n93: \n94: ## 4. 精度要求\n95: \n96: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n97: \n98: **误差指标**：\n99: \n100: 1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n101: \n102:    $$\n103:    \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n104:    $$\n105: \n106: 2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n107: \n108:    $$\n109:    \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n110:    $$\n111: \n112: **通过标准**：\n113: \n114: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n115: |----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n116: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n117: \n118: 当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n119: \n120: \n121: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n122: \n123: ```python\n124: import torch\n125: from typing import List\n126: \n127: def foreach_norm(\n128:     x: List[torch.Tensor], scalar: float\n129: ) -> List[torch.Tensor]:\n130:     \"\"\"\n131:     对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n132: \n133:     公式：y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n134: \n135:     Args:\n136:         x: 输入张量列表 (TensorList)\n137:         scalar: 范数阶数\n138: \n139:     Returns:\n140:         输出张量列表，每个张量的范数结果\n141:     \"\"\"\n142: \n143:     # 检测输入 dtype\n144:     input_dtype = x[0].dtype if x else torch.float32\n145: \n146:     # FP16/BF16 输入需要升到 FP32 计算以保证精度\n147:     # FP32/FP64 输入保持原样计算\n148:     if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n149:         compute_dtype = torch.float32\n150:     else:\n151:         compute_dtype = input_dtype\n152: \n153:     # 转换到计算精度\n154:     x_compute = [t.to(compute_dtype) for t in x]\n155: \n156:     y = [torch.norm(tensor, p=scalar) for tensor in x_compute]\n157: \n158:     # 转回原始 dtype\n159:     if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n160:         return [t.to(input_dtype) for t in y]\n161:     return y\n162: ```\n163: \n164: ## 6. 额外信息\n165: \n166: ### 算子调用示例\n167: \n168: ```python\n169: import torch\n170: import cann_bench\n171: \n172: t1 = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n173: t2 = torch.randn(2048, 512, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n174: y = cann_bench.foreach_norm([t1, t2], scalar=2.0)  # L2 范数\n175: ```\n176: \n177: ## proto.yaml\n178: \n179: ```yaml\n180: operator:\n181:   name: ForeachNorm\n182:   category: Reduction\n183:   difficulty: L1\n184:   formula: y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n185:   description: 对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n186:   shape_support: 输入 ND 格式\n187:   attrs:\n188:   - name: scalar\n189:     type: float\n190:     description: 范数阶数\n191:     required: true\n192:   inputs:\n193:   - name: x\n194:     description: 输入张量列表 (TensorList)\n195:     dtype:\n196:     - float32\n197:     - float16\n198:     - bfloat16\n199:     is_list: true\n200:   outputs:\n201:   - name: y\n202:     description: 输出张量，范数结果\n203:     dtype:\n204:     - float32\n205:     - float16\n206:     - bfloat16\n207:   schema: foreach_norm(Tensor[] x, float scalar) -> Tensor[] y\n208: ```\n209: \n210: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n211: \n212: ```python\n213: #!/usr/bin/python3\n214: # coding=utf-8\n215: \n216: \n217: import torch\n218: from typing import List\n219: \n220: \"\"\"\n221: ForeachNorm 算子 Torch Golden 参考实现\n222: \n223: 对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n224: 公式：y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n225: \"\"\"\n226: def foreach_norm(\n227:     x: List[torch.Tensor], scalar: float\n228: ) -> List[torch.Tensor]:\n229:     \"\"\"\n230:     对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n231: \n232:     公式：y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n233: \n234:     Args:\n235:         x: 输入张量列表 (TensorList)\n236:         scalar: 范数阶数\n237: \n238:     Returns:\n239:         输出张量列表，每个张量的范数结果\n240:     \"\"\"\n241:     # 检测输入 dtype\n242:     input_dtype = x[0].dtype if x else torch.float32\n243: \n244:     # FP16/BF16 输入需要升到 FP32 计算以保证精度\n245:     # FP32/FP64 输入保持原样计算\n246:     if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n247:         compute_dtype = torch.float32\n248:     else:\n249:         compute_dtype = input_dtype\n250: \n251:     # 转换到计算精度\n252:     x_compute = [t.to(compute_dtype) for t in x]\n253: \n254:     y = [torch.norm(tensor, p=scalar) for tensor in x_compute]\n255: \n256:     # 转回原始 dtype\n257:     if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n258:         return [t.to(input_dtype) for t in y]\n259:     return y\n260: ```\n261: \n262: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n263: \n264: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n265: |---|---|---|---|---|\n266: | 1 | [[[1024, 1024], [1024, 1024]]] | ['float16'] | [-1.0, 1.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n267: | 2 | [[[2048, 2048], [2048, 2048], [2048, 2048]]] | ['float32'] | [-2.0, 2.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n268: | 3 | [[[4096, 4096]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3.0, 3.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n269: | 4 | [[[2048, 2048]]] | ['float16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n270: | 5 | [[[2048, 4096], [2048, 4096], [2048, 4096]]] | ['float32'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'scalar': 3.0} |\n271: | 6 | [[[1023, 1023]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'scalar': 1.5} |\n272: | 7 | [[[1009, 1021]]] | ['float16'] | [-1.0, 2.0] | {'scalar': 1.5} |\n273: | 8 | [[[1537, 769]]] | ['float32'] | [-5.0, 10.0] | {'scalar': 4.0} |\n274: | 9 | [[[363, 367, 373], [363, 367, 373]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50.0, 100.0] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n275: | 10 | [[[2049, 513]]] | ['float16'] | [-65504.0, 65504.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n276: | 11 | [[[3, 7, 13, 4001], [3, 7, 13, 4001], [3, 7, 13, 4001]]] | ['float32'] | [-88.0, 88.0] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n277: | 12 | [[[1000003]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | {'scalar': inf} |\n278: | 13 | [[[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]]] | ['float32'] | [-10.0, 10.0] | {'scalar': 5.0} |\n279: | 14 | [[[3, 7, 11, 13, 1009]]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n280: | 15 | [[[512, 2049], [512, 2049]]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n281: | 16 | [[[255, 8193], [255, 8193], [255, 8193], [255, 8193]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1.0, 3.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n282: | 17 | [[[4097, 511]]] | ['float16'] | [-1000.0, 1000.0] | {'scalar': -1.0} |\n283: | 18 | [[[2, 511, 2049], [2, 511, 2049]]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n284: | 19 | [[[4, 255, 2049], [4, 255, 2049]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3.0, 6.0] | {'scalar': 3.0} |\n285: | 20 | [[[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]]] | ['float32'] | [-20.0, 40.0] | {'scalar': 2.5} |\n286: \n287: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n288: \n289: ```python\n290: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n291: \n292: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n293:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n294:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n295:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n296:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n297:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n298:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n299:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n300:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n301:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n302: \"\"\"\n303: \n304: import torch\n305: \n306: import asc\n307: import asc2\n308: \n309: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n310: \n311: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n312: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n313: _MAX_CORES = 72\n314: \n315: \n316: @asc2.jit\n317: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n318:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n319:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n320:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n321:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n322:                         unroll_factor=2):\n323:         off = t * tile_size\n324:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n325:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n326:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n327:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n328:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n329: \n330: \n331: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n332:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n333:     ensure_npu_platform()\n334:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n335:         x = x.contiguous()\n336:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n337:     size = x.numel()\n338:     if size == 0:\n339:         return out\n340:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n341:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n342:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n343:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n344:     else:\n345:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n346:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n347:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n348:     return out\n349: \n350: ```\n351: \n352: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n353: \n354: ## Module shape\n355: \n356: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n357: \n358: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n359:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n360: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n361: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n362: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n363:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n364:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n365:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n366:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n367: \n368: ## Kernel authoring rules\n369: \n370: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n371:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n372:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n373: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n374:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n375:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n376: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n377: \n378: ```python\n379: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n380:     off = t * tile_size\n381:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n382:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n383:     ...compute on tiles...\n384:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n385: ```\n386: \n387: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n388:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n389: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n390:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n391:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n392:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n393:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n394:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n395:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n396:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n397:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n398:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n399:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n400: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n401:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n402:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n403: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n404:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n405: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n406:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n407: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n408:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n409:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n410:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n411:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n412:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n413:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n414:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n415:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n416:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n417:   cases).\n418: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n419:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n420: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n421:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n422:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n423:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n424:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n425:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n426:   TypeError).\n427: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n428:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n429:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n430:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n431:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n432:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n433:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n434: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n435:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n436:   style (min 32 bytes).\n437: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n438:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n439:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n440: \n441: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n442: \n443: - Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n444:   nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n445:   `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n446: - Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n447:   - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n448:   - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n449:   - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n450:     `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n451:     for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n452:   - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n453:     `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n454:     the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n455: - IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n456:   through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n457:   unless the golden does.\n458: \n459: ## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n460: \n461: - ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n462: - torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n463:   `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n464:   `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n465:   `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n466: - FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n467:   `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n468:   `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n469:   `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n470:   rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n471:   is detected and scored as cheating.\n472: - Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n473:   shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n474: \n475: \n476: # Operator-specific guidance\n477: \n478: - Signature: foreach_norm(x: List[Tensor], scalar: float) -> List[Tensor];\n479:   per entry a FULL reduction to a 0-dim tensor: y = (sum |v|^p)^(1/p) with\n480:   p = scalar in {-1.0, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, inf}.\n481: - Choose the reduction form on the HOST per p (it is a Python float):\n482:   p == 1 -> sum(abs(v)); p == 2 -> sqrt(sum(v*v)); p == inf -> max(abs(v));\n483:   otherwise -> S = sum(exp(log(abs(v)) * p)) and result = exp(log(S) / p).\n484:   log(0) = -inf propagates correctly through exp — do not clamp.\n485: - Parallel reduction (both building blocks VERIFIED on this hardware):\n486:   grid-stride kernel where each core keeps a loop-carried accumulator (seed\n487:   with asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)), then\n488:   acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(...) in a PLAIN asc2.range loop — do NOT pass\n489:   gm_barrier, it does not exist on this build) and finally\n490:   asc2.atomic_add(asc2.full([8], acc, dtype=asc.float32), out_gm, [0]) to\n491:   combine across cores (host zero-seeds via torch.zeros — tensor creation\n492:   is allowed). For p == inf there is no atomic max for the final combine\n493:   from all cores — either use asc2.atomic_max (exists, same signature) or\n494:   per-core slots + a second single-core pass. Apply the final power in a\n495:   second tiny single-core kernel reading the accumulated sum. Host returns\n496:   out8[0] (a 0-dim view of the result buffer) — matches torch.norm's\n497:   output shape/dtype.\n498: - Accumulate in f32; cast the final scalar back to the input dtype.\n499: \n500: # Deliverable\n501: \n502: Write ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `foreach_norm` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n503: \n504: IMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.\n\n(End of file - total 504 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **ForeachNorm**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `foreach_norm` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# ForeachNorm 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nForeachNorm 算子对输入张量列表（TensorList）的每个张量分别进行范数运算，支持多种范数阶数。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- 梯度裁剪中的梯度范数计算\n- 优化器中的参数范数监控\n- 模型正则化中的权重范数约束","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/task.md","text":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **ForeachNorm**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `foreach_norm` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# ForeachNorm 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nForeachNorm 算子对输入张量列表（TensorList）的每个张量分别进行范数运算，支持多种范数阶数。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- 梯度裁剪中的梯度范数计算\n- 优化器中的参数范数监控\n- 模型正则化中的权重范数约束\n\n**算子特征**：\n- 难度等级：L1（Reduction）\n- 输入为张量列表，对每个张量独立计算范数，输出为标量张量列表\n- 支持 ND 格式输入\n\n## 2. 算子定义\n\n### 数学公式\n\n**通用 p 范数**：\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\sum_i |x_i|^p\\right)^{1/p}\n$$\n\n### 常见范数\n\n| 范数阶数 (scalar) | 公式 | 含义 |\n|-------------------|------|------|\n| 0 | $\\sum_i \\mathbb{1}(x_i \\neq 0)$ | L0 范数（非零元素个数） |\n| 1 | $\\sum_i \\|x_i\\|$ | L1 范数（绝对值之和） |\n| 2 | $\\sqrt{\\sum_i x_i^2}$ | L2 范数（欧氏距离） |\n| inf | $\\max_i \\|x_i\\|$ | 无穷范数（最大绝对值） |\n\n## 3. 接口规范\n\n### 算子原型\n\n```python\ncann_bench.foreach_norm(Tensor[] x, float scalar) -> Tensor[] y\n```\n\n### 输入参数说明\n\n| 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n|------|------|--------|------|\n| x | Tensor[] | 必选 | 输入张量列表（TensorList） |\n| scalar | float | 必选 | 范数阶数 |\n\n### 输出\n\n| 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n|------|-------|-------|------|\n| y | 每个元素为标量张量 | 与输入 dtype 相同 | 每个输入张量的范数结果列表 |\n\n### 数据类型\n\n| 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| float16 | float16 |\n| float32 | float32 |\n| bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n\n### 规则与约束\n\n- 输入为 TensorList，列表中每个张量独立计算范数\n- 列表中各张量的 dtype 须一致\n- `scalar` 支持正数、负数、0、inf 等值\n- 负阶范数要求输入元素不为零\n\n### 支持范围\n\n输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n\n| 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n|---|---|---|\n| TensorList 长度（`L`） | 1 ~ 64 | cases.csv 实测 1 ~ 4 |\n| 每个张量维度数 | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1D ~ 5D |\n| 每个张量各维大小 | 1 ~ 1048576 | cases.csv 各维实测 2 ~ 1000003 |\n| 每个张量元素总数 | 1 ~ 48M | cases.csv 每个张量实测 ~1M ~ ~47M |\n| `scalar`（范数阶数） | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 -1.0 ~ 5.0（含 inf）；负阶或 0 阶时输入元素须非零 |\n\n## 4. 精度要求\n\n采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n\n**误差指标**：\n\n1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n**通过标准**：\n\n| 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n|----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n\n当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n\n\n## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef foreach_norm(\n    x: List[torch.Tensor], scalar: float\n) -> List[torch.Tensor]:\n    \"\"\"\n    对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n\n    公式：y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n\n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量列表 (TensorList)\n        scalar: 范数阶数\n\n    Returns:\n        输出张量列表，每个张量的范数结果\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # 检测输入 dtype\n    input_dtype = x[0].dtype if x else torch.float32\n\n    # FP16/BF16 输入需要升到 FP32 计算以保证精度\n    # FP32/FP64 输入保持原样计算\n    if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n        compute_dtype = torch.float32\n    else:\n        compute_dtype = input_dtype\n\n    # 转换到计算精度\n    x_compute = [t.to(compute_dtype) for t in x]\n\n    y = [torch.norm(tensor, p=scalar) for tensor in x_compute]\n\n    # 转回原始 dtype\n    if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n        return [t.to(input_dtype) for t in y]\n    return y\n```\n\n## 6. 额外信息\n\n### 算子调用示例\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport cann_bench\n\nt1 = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\nt2 = torch.randn(2048, 512, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\ny = cann_bench.foreach_norm([t1, t2], scalar=2.0)  # L2 范数\n```\n\n## proto.yaml\n\n```yaml\noperator:\n  name: ForeachNorm\n  category: Reduction\n  difficulty: L1\n  formula: y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n  description: 对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n  shape_support: 输入 ND 格式\n  attrs:\n  - name: scalar\n    type: float\n    description: 范数阶数\n    required: true\n  inputs:\n  - name: x\n    description: 输入张量列表 (TensorList)\n    dtype:\n    - float32\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n    is_list: true\n  outputs:\n  - name: y\n    description: 输出张量，范数结果\n    dtype:\n    - float32\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n  schema: foreach_norm(Tensor[] x, float scalar) -> Tensor[] y\n```\n\n## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/python3\n# coding=utf-8\n\n\nimport torch\nfrom typing import List\n\n\"\"\"\nForeachNorm 算子 Torch Golden 参考实现\n\n对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n公式：y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n\"\"\"\ndef foreach_norm(\n    x: List[torch.Tensor], scalar: float\n) -> List[torch.Tensor]:\n    \"\"\"\n    对输入张量列表的每个张量进行范数运算\n\n    公式：y = (sum |x_i|^p)^(1/p)\n\n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量列表 (TensorList)\n        scalar: 范数阶数\n\n    Returns:\n        输出张量列表，每个张量的范数结果\n    \"\"\"\n    # 检测输入 dtype\n    input_dtype = x[0].dtype if x else torch.float32\n\n    # FP16/BF16 输入需要升到 FP32 计算以保证精度\n    # FP32/FP64 输入保持原样计算\n    if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n        compute_dtype = torch.float32\n    else:\n        compute_dtype = input_dtype\n\n    # 转换到计算精度\n    x_compute = [t.to(compute_dtype) for t in x]\n\n    y = [torch.norm(tensor, p=scalar) for tensor in x_compute]\n\n    # 转回原始 dtype\n    if input_dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):\n        return [t.to(input_dtype) for t in y]\n    return y\n```\n\n## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n\n| case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [[[1024, 1024], [1024, 1024]]] | ['float16'] | [-1.0, 1.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n| 2 | [[[2048, 2048], [2048, 2048], [2048, 2048]]] | ['float32'] | [-2.0, 2.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n| 3 | [[[4096, 4096]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3.0, 3.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n| 4 | [[[2048, 2048]]] | ['float16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n| 5 | [[[2048, 4096], [2048, 4096], [2048, 4096]]] | ['float32'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'scalar': 3.0} |\n| 6 | [[[1023, 1023]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'scalar': 1.5} |\n| 7 | [[[1009, 1021]]] | ['float16'] | [-1.0, 2.0] | {'scalar': 1.5} |\n| 8 | [[[1537, 769]]] | ['float32'] | [-5.0, 10.0] | {'scalar': 4.0} |\n| 9 | [[[363, 367, 373], [363, 367, 373]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50.0, 100.0] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n| 10 | [[[2049, 513]]] | ['float16'] | [-65504.0, 65504.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n| 11 | [[[3, 7, 13, 4001], [3, 7, 13, 4001], [3, 7, 13, 4001]]] | ['float32'] | [-88.0, 88.0] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n| 12 | [[[1000003]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | {'scalar': inf} |\n| 13 | [[[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]]] | ['float32'] | [-10.0, 10.0] | {'scalar': 5.0} |\n| 14 | [[[3, 7, 11, 13, 1009]]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n| 15 | [[[512, 2049], [512, 2049]]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n| 16 | [[[255, 8193], [255, 8193], [255, 8193], [255, 8193]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1.0, 3.0] | {'scalar': 1.0} |\n| 17 | [[[4097, 511]]] | ['float16'] | [-1000.0, 1000.0] | {'scalar': -1.0} |\n| 18 | [[[2, 511, 2049], [2, 511, 2049]]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'scalar': 2.0} |\n| 19 | [[[4, 255, 2049], [4, 255, 2049]]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3.0, 6.0] | {'scalar': 3.0} |\n| 20 | [[[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]]] | ['float32'] | [-20.0, 40.0] | {'scalar': 2.5} |\n\n# Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n\n```python\n\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n\n_WIDE_TILE = 3072\n_NARROW_TILE = 1024\n_MAX_CORES = 72\n\n\n@asc2.jit\ndef _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                    size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        off = t * tile_size\n        n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n        x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n        xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n        y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n        asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n\n\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    if not x.is_contiguous():\n        x = x.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0:\n        return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out\n\n```\n\n# pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n\n## Module shape\n\nYour file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n\n- imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n  `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n- one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n- ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n- wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n  needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n  `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n  launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n  return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n\n## Kernel authoring rules\n\n- Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n  `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n  copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n- Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n  (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n  value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n- Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n\n```python\nfor t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    off = t * tile_size\n    n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n    x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n    ...compute on tiles...\n    asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n```\n\n- Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n  on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n- Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n  `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n  `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n  `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n  comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n  `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n  `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n  `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n  `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n  `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n  `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n- int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n  (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n  no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n- Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n  write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n- f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n  compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n- UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n  Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n  doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n  calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n  `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n  311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n  `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n  Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n  512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n  bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n  cases).\n- `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n  (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n- Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n  `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n  (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n  different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n  `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n  `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n  TypeError).\n- Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n  [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n  int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n  destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n  Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n  `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n  kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n- Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n  `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n  style (min 32 bytes).\n- Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n  `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n  `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n\n## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n\n- Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n  nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n  `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n- Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n  - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n  - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n  - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n    `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n    for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n  - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n    `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n    the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n- IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n  through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n  unless the golden does.\n\n## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n\n- ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n- torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n  `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n  `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n  `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n- FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n  `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n  `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n  `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n  rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n  is detected and scored as cheating.\n- Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n  shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n\n\n# Operator-specific guidance\n\n- Signature: foreach_norm(x: List[Tensor], scalar: float) -> List[Tensor];\n  per entry a FULL reduction to a 0-dim tensor: y = (sum |v|^p)^(1/p) with\n  p = scalar in {-1.0, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, inf}.\n- Choose the reduction form on the HOST per p (it is a Python float):\n  p == 1 -> sum(abs(v)); p == 2 -> sqrt(sum(v*v)); p == inf -> max(abs(v));\n  otherwise -> S = sum(exp(log(abs(v)) * p)) and result = exp(log(S) / p).\n  log(0) = -inf propagates correctly through exp — do not clamp.\n- Parallel reduction (both building blocks VERIFIED on this hardware):\n  grid-stride kernel where each core keeps a loop-carried accumulator (seed\n  with asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)), then\n  acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(...) in a PLAIN asc2.range loop — do NOT pass\n  gm_barrier, it does not exist on this build) and finally\n  asc2.atomic_add(asc2.full([8], acc, dtype=asc.float32), out_gm, [0]) to\n  combine across cores (host zero-seeds via torch.zeros — tensor creation\n  is allowed). For p == inf there is no atomic max for the final combine\n  from all cores — either use asc2.atomic_max (exists, same signature) or\n  per-core slots + a second single-core pass. Apply the final power in a\n  second tiny single-core kernel reading the accumulated sum. Host returns\n  out8[0] (a 0-dim view of the result buffer) — matches torch.norm's\n  output shape/dtype.\n- Accumulate in f32; cast the final scalar back to the input dtype.\n\n# Deliverable\n\nWrite ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `foreach_norm` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n\nIMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":504,"totalLines":504,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/task.md","time":{"start":1788348720344,"end":1788348720351}},"id":"prt_061e3e282001sXdU8gI7I8ai9f","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","messageID":"msg_061e3cf9b001kHp8tjYdrL7JaT"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788348720418,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"id":"prt_061e3e51b0011VqCvD1zS34KZq","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"","messageID":"msg_061e3cf9b001kHp8tjYdrL7JaT","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":9208,"input":4720,"output":131,"reasoning":133,"cache":{"write":0,"read":4224}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788348724128,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"id":"prt_061e3f39a001wxE5qjlLBk3DwS","messageID":"msg_061e3e521001YV15zgiQirNTd7","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348826140,"sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_0f058c1df19b4cef956034e9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/design.md","content":"# ForeachNorm Design\n\n## Algorithm\n\nEach tensor in the input list is independently reduced to a scalar p-norm:\n`y = (sum |v|^p)^(1/p)`. Host iterates the list, launching kernels per tensor.\n\nDispatch by scalar p (host-side, p is a Python float):\n\n| p | In-kernel per-tile op | Final transform (pass 2) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1.0 | `asc2.reduce_sum(abs(xf))` | none (identity) |\n| 2.0 | `asc2.reduce_sum(xf * xf)` | `sqrt(S)` |\n| inf | `asc2.reduce_max(abs(xf))` | none (identity) |\n| -1.0 | `asc2.reduce_sum(exp(log(abs(xf)) * (-1.0)))` | `1.0 / S` via `exp(log(S) * (-1.0))` |\n| general (1.5, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0) | `asc2.reduce_sum(exp(log(abs(xf)) * p))` | `exp(log(S) / p)` |\n\nTwo-pass architecture per tensor:\n- **Pass 1** (multi-core): grid-stride reduction; each core accumulates partial sum/max across its tiles, then `asc2.atomic_add` (or `asc2.atomic_max` for p=inf) a widened scalar into `out_gm[0]`. Host zero-seeds with `torch.zeros(8, dtype=f32)`.\n- **Pass 2** (single-core, 1 tile): reads accumulated S, applies final transform, writes result. For p==1 and p==inf the final transform is identity — pass 2 still runs but just copies, keeping the code path uniform.\n\n## Pinned-v2 APIs\n\n- `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` for 1-D GM views\n- `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [TILE], real_shape=[n])` / `asc2.copy_out(tile, gm, [off], real_shape=[n])`\n- `asc2.range(start, stop, step, unroll_factor=2)` — NO `gm_barrier`, NO `parallel`\n- `asc2.reduce_sum`, `asc2.reduce_max` for tile → scalar\n- `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm, [offset])`, `asc2.atomic_max(src_tile, dst_gm, [offset])`\n- `asc2.full([8], scalar, dtype=asc.float32)` for scalar widening before atomic\n- `asc2.abs`, `asc2.sqrt`, `asc2.exp`, `asc2.log` for element-wise\n- `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for tile_size (compile-time)\n- `.to(asc.float32)` / `.to(x.dtype)` for dtype promotion/cast-back\n- Scalar accumulator seed: `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n\n## All 20 Cases Coverage\n\n| Case | Shapes (nelem) | dtype | p | Special notes |\n|------|---------------|-------|---|--------------|\n| 1 | 2x [1024,1024] ~1M | f16 | 1.0 | L1 path |\n| 2 | 3x [2048,2048] ~4M | f32 | 1.0 | L1 path, no promotion needed |\n| 3 | 1x [4096,4096] ~16M | bf16 | 1.0 | L1 path, large tensor |\n| 4 | 1x [2048,2048] ~4M | f16 | 2.0 | L2 path |\n| 5 | 3x [2048,4096] ~8M | f32 | 3.0 | General path |\n| 6 | 1x [1023,1023] ~1M | bf16 | 1.5 | General path, odd dims |\n| 7 | 1x [1009,1021] ~1M | f16 | 1.5 | General path, non-power-of-2 |\n| 8 | 1x [1537,769] ~1.2M | f32 | 4.0 | General path |\n| 9 | 2x [363,367,373] ~50M | bf16 | 2.0 | L2 path, 3D |\n| 10 | 1x [2049,513] ~1M | f16 | 1.0 | Full f16 range [-65504, 65504] |\n| 11 | 3x [3,7,13,4001] ~1M | f32 | 2.0 | L2 path, 4D |\n| 12 | 1x [1000003] ~1M | bf16 | inf | Inf-norm, values include +/-inf |\n| 13 | 1x [11,13,17,67,67] ~1.7M | f32 | 5.0 | General path, 5D |\n| 14 | 1x [3,7,11,13,1009] ~3M | f16 | 2.0 | **All-zero input** → sum=0, sqrt(0)=0 |\n| 15 | 2x [512,2049] ~1M | f32 | 2.0 | L2 path |\n| 16 | 4x [255,8193] ~2M | bf16 | 1.0 | L1 path, 4 tensors |\n| 17 | 1x [4097,511] ~2M | f16 | -1.0 | Negative p; spec guarantees nonzero elements |\n| 18 | 2x [2,511,2049] ~2M | f32 | 2.0 | L2 path, 3D |\n| 19 | 2x [4,255,2049] ~2M | bf16 | 3.0 | General path, 3D |\n| 20 | 4x [2,3,17,1024,101] ~10.5M | f32 | 2.5 | General path, 5D, large |\n\n## Tiling\n\n- **TILE = 2048** for all paths. Simple paths (p=1,2,inf) could use 4096 but 2048 keeps one kernel specialization and is safe for the general log-exp chain.\n- Tile size is `asc.ConstExpr[int]` — baked at compile time.\n- For small tensors (< TILE), single tile with real_shape handles it.\n- Grid stride: `num_tiles = ceildiv(size, TILE)`, `cores = min(72, num_tiles)`.\n\n## Tails\n\n- `n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off`\n- `real_shape=[n]` on both `copy_in` and `copy_out`\n- No host padding; partial tiles are handled natively via real_shape.\n- All case shapes are non-aligned (e.g., 1009*1021, 1537*769, 1000003) — tails are exercised in nearly every case.\n\n## UB Budget\n\nMeasured calibration: sigmoid chain (6 f32 values, unroll=2, TILE=2048) = 155648 bytes. Factor ≈ 1.6x over naive `visible * 4 * TILE * 2`.\n\n**Pass-1 general kernel** (worst case, p not in {1, 2, inf}):\n- Live f32 tiles: xf (cast), abs(xf), log(|xf|), log(|xf|)*p, exp(log(|xf|)*p) = 5 tiles\n- Naive: 5 * 4 * 2048 * 2 = 81920 bytes\n- Estimated with 1.6x: ~131072 bytes\n- Plus accumulator seed tile + atomic widen tile: +2*4*8 = negligible\n- **Total ~135 KB < 253952 B — FITS**\n\n**Pass-1 simple kernels** (p=1, p=2, p=inf):\n- Live f32 tiles: xf, abs/xf*xf = 2-3 tiles\n- Estimated: ~80-100 KB — comfortable margin\n\n**Pass-2 kernel** (single-core, [8] tile):\n- Trivial: one 8-element tile — negligible UB\n\n**Verdict**: TILE=2048 is safe for all paths. No UB overflow risk.\n\n## Numerical Risks\n\n1. **log(0) = -inf**: For general p path, if element is 0: log(0) = -inf, -inf * p = -inf (p>0) or +inf (p<0), exp(-inf)=0 or exp(+inf)=inf. Case 14 has all zeros with p=2 — but p=2 uses the sqrt(sum(v*v)) path, not general. Safe. No case has zeros with general-p kernel.\n2. **Overflow in exp(log(|v|)*p)**: Max |v| across general cases:\n   - Case 5: |v|≤0.1, p=3 → exp(log(0.1)*3)=exp(-6.9)≈0.001. Safe.\n   - Case 8: |v|≤10, p=4 → exp(log(10)*4)=exp(9.21)≈10000. Sum over 1.2M → ~1.2e10. log(1.2e10)≈23.9, /4=5.98, exp(5.98)≈395. f32 safe.\n   - Case 13: |v|≤10, p=5 → exp(log(10)*5)=exp(11.51)≈100000. Sum over 1.7M → ~1.7e11. log=25.86, /5=5.17, exp(5.17)≈176. Safe.\n   - Case 17: p=-1 → exp(log(|v|)*(-1))=1/|v|. |v|∈(0,1000]. Min nonzero → large but sum is bounded. f32 range sufficient.\n   - Case 19: |v|≤6, p=3 → exp(log(6)*3)=exp(5.37)≈216. Safe.\n   - Case 20: |v|≤40, p=2.5 → exp(log(40)*2.5)=exp(9.22)≈10100. Sum over 10.5M → ~1e11. f32 safe.\n3. **Accumulator overflow (f32)**: Max partial sum per core ≈ total_sum / 72. Worst case: case 9 with p=2, |v|≤100, 50M elements → sum ≈ 50M * 10000 = 5e11. f32 holds ~3.4e38. Safe.\n4. **Catastrophic cancellation**: Not applicable — we use sum of non-negative terms (|v|^p ≥ 0). No subtraction.\n5. **sqrt(0)**: Case 14 all-zeros. sqrt(0)=0. Correct.\n6. **inf inputs (case 12)**: max(|inf|) = inf. IEEE propagation is correct. For bf16, inf is representable.\n7. **f16 precision**: All compute in f32, cast final scalar back to input dtype. Threshold: f16 = 2^-10 ≈ 9.8e-4, bf16 = 2^-7 ≈ 7.8e-3, f32 = 2^-13 ≈ 1.2e-4. f32 accumulation gives ~6-7 digits, well within all thresholds.\n\n## Anti-Cheat Constraints\n\n- ALL math inside `@asc2.jit` kernels\n- torch used ONLY for: `torch.zeros`/`torch.empty` allocation, `.numel()`, `.dtype`, `.device`, `.is_contiguous()`, `.contiguous()`, `.view()`\n- NO `torch.norm`, NO tensor arithmetic on host, NO `.to(dtype)` on device data\n- Host iterates list, launches kernels per tensor, returns list of 0-dim tensors\n- `ensure_npu_platform()` called at top of public function\n- Output is freshly allocated (not views of input)\n\n## Local Validation Ladder\n\n1. **Syntax gate**: `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py`\n2. **Static contract check**: worker harness validates module shape, imports, callable signature\n3. **Exact-v2 compile gate**: `python3 -c \"import candidate\"` with pinned pyasc v2 — all 20 case routes must lower through JIT without compile error\n4. **No numerical verification**: no NPU/camodel available; evidence label is `verified-local-compile` at best\n5. **Submission requires**: camodel for numerical evidence, CANNBench on real NPU for acceptance\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/design.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/foreach_norm-generate/design.md","time":{"start":1788348826133,"end":1788348826138}},"id":"prt_061e4d0f9001qlp27Qp2R9gjjM","sessionID":"ses_f9e1c3132ffehEpoOb7WAjFZza","messageID":"msg_061e3e521001YV15zgiQirNTd7"}}
